{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/340150-missing-dark-kingdom","id":340150,"title":"Missing Dark Kingdom?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Wraith: The Oblivion"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-12-14T11:31:13-08:00","id":340150,"title":"Missing Dark Kingdom?","content":"I was thinking about the territory that Stygia claims, and how the Middle East is supposed to fall under its domain via Roman Imperial conquest. However, the Roman Empire (including their Byzantine successors... who of course considered themselves the same Empire) never extended that far East, or even bothered to claim the bulk of the Arabian Peninsula (it was ignored as valueless desert at the time). Even if we accept that North Africa and Asia Minor Shadowlands remained under Stygian control even after the rise of future Caliphates and the Ottomans in the Skinlands, that still leaves the historic territory of the Sassanid Persians as a huge blank space on the map that includes modern Iran, Iraq, and a massive chunk of Central Asia.<br /> <br /> Aren't we missing AT LEAST ONE Dark Kingdom, descended from the Persian Empire, the way Stygia claims descent from Rome and the Yellow Springs from the Qin? If not more than one, since I find it hard to imagine the countless Muslim dead over the centuries accepting Stygian &quot;Crusader&quot; rule in territories like North Africa and the Levant.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-14T11:40:29-08:00","id":340158,"title":"","content":"Hmm... I never thought about that but you're right. There's absolutely nothing on the lands of the dead for the Middle East which is strange considering all the Dark Kingdoms we do have as well as the material on the Middle East in other CWoD lines.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://youtu.be/_yBS2EZFduI?t=2m13s\" target=\"_blank\">Homo sapiens. What an inventive, invincible species. It's only a few million years since they crawled up out of the mud and learned to walk. Puny, defenceless bipeds. They've survived flood, famine and plague. They've survived cosmic wars and holocausts. And now, here they are, out among the stars, waiting to begin a new life. Ready to outsit eternity. They're indomitable. Indomitable.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/420-akatsukileader13","name":"AkatsukiLeader13","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar420_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1637"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-14T11:55:37-08:00","id":340164,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>glamourweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/340150-missing-dark-kingdom#post340150\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I was thinking about the territory that Stygia claims, and how the Middle East is supposed to fall under its domain via Roman Imperial conquest. However, the Roman Empire (including their Byzantine successors... who of course considered themselves the same Empire) never extended that far East, or even bothered to claim the bulk of the Arabian Peninsula (it was ignored as valueless desert at the time). Even if we accept that North Africa and Asia Minor Shadowlands remained under Stygian control even after the rise of future Caliphates and the Ottomans in the Skinlands, that still leaves the historic territory of the Sassanid Persians as a huge blank space on the map that includes modern Iran, Iraq, and a massive chunk of Central Asia.<br /> <br /> Aren't we missing AT LEAST ONE Dark Kingdom, descended from the Persian Empire, the way Stygia claims descent from Rome and the Yellow Springs from the Qin? If not more than one, since I find it hard to imagine the countless Muslim dead over the centuries accepting Stygian &quot;Crusader&quot; rule in territories like North Africa and the Levant.</div> </div> </div> </div>Rich Dansky was asked about the Middle Eastern Shadowlands in &quot;Ask the Deadguy&quot;, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/34987-ask-the-deadguy?p=48673#post48673\">and said he couldn't answer because of spoilers</a>.<br /> <br /> In Ends of Empire, the Egyptian Underworld has a treaty with the Ferrymen, involving mutual non-aggression and the Ferrymen's assistance against enemies, which was approved by Charon.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/51-marin","name":"marin","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2019"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-14T12:15:29-08:00","id":340170,"title":"","content":"By Egyptian Underworld though, you mean the Far Shore where Mummies hang out right?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-14T12:36:15-08:00","id":340175,"title":"","content":"No, the Dark Kingdom of Sand. You see the God Anubis struck a deal with the Ferrymen a long, long time ago. In exchange for secret knowledge on the nature of the soul, which they would use to create the Ritual of Separation, the Ferrymen would have Stygia vow to never invade the Dark Kingdom of Sand and leave it alone, as well as a favor to be called in latter.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://youtu.be/_yBS2EZFduI?t=2m13s\" target=\"_blank\">Homo sapiens. What an inventive, invincible species. It's only a few million years since they crawled up out of the mud and learned to walk. Puny, defenceless bipeds. They've survived flood, famine and plague. They've survived cosmic wars and holocausts. And now, here they are, out among the stars, waiting to begin a new life. Ready to outsit eternity. They're indomitable. Indomitable.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/420-akatsukileader13","name":"AkatsukiLeader13","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar420_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1637"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-14T15:06:20-08:00","id":340247,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>AkatsukiLeader13</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/340150-missing-dark-kingdom?p=340175#post340175\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">No, the Dark Kingdom of Sand. You see the God Anubis struck a deal with the Ferrymen a long, long time ago. In exchange for secret knowledge on the nature of the soul, which they would use to create the Ritual of Separation, the Ferrymen would have Stygia vow to never invade the Dark Kingdom of Sand and leave it alone, as well as a favor to be called in latter.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> My understanding was that &quot;Amenti&quot;/The Dark Kingdom of Sand was specifically a realm in the Tempest like Stygia and the Far Shores - they didn't control the Shadowlands of modern Egypt.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-14T15:54:27-08:00","id":340266,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>glamourweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/340150-missing-dark-kingdom#post340150\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I was thinking about the territory that Stygia claims, and how the Middle East is supposed to fall under its domain via Roman Imperial conquest. However, the Roman Empire (including their Byzantine successors... who of course considered themselves the same Empire) never extended that far East, or even bothered to claim the bulk of the Arabian Peninsula (it was ignored as valueless desert at the time). Even if we accept that North Africa and Asia Minor Shadowlands remained under Stygian control even after the rise of future Caliphates and the Ottomans in the Skinlands, that still leaves the historic territory of the Sassanid Persians as a huge blank space on the map that includes modern Iran, Iraq, and a massive chunk of Central Asia.<br /> <br /> Aren't we missing AT LEAST ONE Dark Kingdom, descended from the Persian Empire, the way Stygia claims descent from Rome and the Yellow Springs from the Qin? If not more than one, since I find it hard to imagine the countless Muslim dead over the centuries accepting Stygian &quot;Crusader&quot; rule in territories like North Africa and the Levant.</div> </div> </div> </div>Perhaps it isn't just Roman sensu stricto that is being contemplated in setting the bounds of Stygia. If you take into consideration Alexander's conquest all the way to India and then his successor's maintaining control of the territory for a chunk of time, that would certainly give Stygia a foothold all the way to Swar. After all, the Sassanids didn't succeed a Persian Dynasty. And, even though Stygia might be Roman, Charon was not. His origins are more definitively Greek (Mycenean even). Maintaining control over Persia might even be something of relative importance. <br /> <br /> In fact, if Stygia = the Roman Empire, then Scotland, Ireland, Scandinavia, Germany eastwards wouldn't be part of the Dark Kingdom of Iron. Historically speaking, there is more of a reason for the Near and Middle East all the way to the borders of Swar would belong to Stygia than any of those. <br /> <br /> It looks like Styiga was great at acquiring territory, but also great at KEEPING it, even after subsequent governmental and cultural changes occurred in the Skinlands in the lands it claimed. .","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-14T16:05:58-08:00","id":340272,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ajax</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/340150-missing-dark-kingdom?p=340266#post340266\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In fact, if Stygia = the Roman Empire, then Scotland, Ireland, Scandinavia, Germany eastwards wouldn't be part of the Dark Kingdom of Iron. Historically speaking, there is more of a reason for the Near and Middle East all the way to the borders of Swar would belong to Stygia than any of those.</div> </div> </div> </div>*points at the Roman and Byzantine Churches, and their cultural influence bringing Classical roots to all of Europe*","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":1098,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","user":"glamourweaver","date":". 12-14-2014, 07:08 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-14T16:07:56-08:00","id":340274,"title":"","content":"Originally, when ancient Egypt was at the height of its power it was a Dark Kingdom, perhaps the first of them all, but as the ancient Egyptian civilization collapsed and other empires rose in the Skinlands, it withered and crumbled to a fraction of what it once was. But it still exists and is considered a Dark Kingdom even if it a mere shadow of one in the present.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://youtu.be/_yBS2EZFduI?t=2m13s\" target=\"_blank\">Homo sapiens. What an inventive, invincible species. It's only a few million years since they crawled up out of the mud and learned to walk. Puny, defenceless bipeds. They've survived flood, famine and plague. They've survived cosmic wars and holocausts. And now, here they are, out among the stars, waiting to begin a new life. Ready to outsit eternity. They're indomitable. Indomitable.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/420-akatsukileader13","name":"AkatsukiLeader13","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar420_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1637"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-14T16:19:36-08:00","id":340281,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>AkatsukiLeader13</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/340150-missing-dark-kingdom?p=340274#post340274\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Originally, when ancient Egypt was at the height of its power it was a Dark Kingdom, perhaps the first of them all, but as the ancient Egyptian civilization collapsed and other empires rose in the Skinlands, it withered and crumbled to a fraction of what it once was. But it still exists and is considered a Dark Kingdom even if it a mere shadow of one in the present.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I understand it's a &quot;Dark Kingdom&quot;, but there are a couple of Dark Kingdoms that don't control Shadowland territory. As far as I'm aware from the Mummy books Anubis, Osiris, and the Ancient Egyptian dead of Amenti command no loyalty of the modern Egyptian dead. What military forces they have are dedicated to the war with Apep and protecting Amenti, not occupying the Cairo Necropolis, etc like how the Stygian Legions operate.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":1098,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","user":"glamourweaver","date":". 12-14-2014, 08:52 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-14T17:42:19-08:00","id":340299,"title":"","content":"Not anymore, yes. But long ago they did, just as the Dark Kingdoms of Obsidian held sway over the Shadowlands of the Americas before the Europeans came. Despite being reduced in power and scope and no longer holding sway over the regions of the Shadowlands they once held sway they still exist in the Underworld.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://youtu.be/_yBS2EZFduI?t=2m13s\" target=\"_blank\">Homo sapiens. What an inventive, invincible species. It's only a few million years since they crawled up out of the mud and learned to walk. Puny, defenceless bipeds. They've survived flood, famine and plague. They've survived cosmic wars and holocausts. And now, here they are, out among the stars, waiting to begin a new life. Ready to outsit eternity. They're indomitable. Indomitable.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/420-akatsukileader13","name":"AkatsukiLeader13","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar420_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1637"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-14T17:52:44-08:00","id":340301,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>AkatsukiLeader13</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/340150-missing-dark-kingdom?p=340299#post340299\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Not anymore, yes. But long ago they did, just as the Dark Kingdoms of Obsidian held sway over the Shadowlands of the Americas before the Europeans came. Despite being reduced in power and scope and no longer holding sway over the regions of the Shadowlands they once held sway they still exist in the Underworld.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Ok, yes we're on the same page then.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> My thinking right now (and what I'd houserule unless Wraith20 has a better answer) is that there are effectively 2 other Dark Kingdoms with Shadowland holdings. One descends from the Persian Empire and lays claim to the dead from Iraq (with echoes of the ancient Mesopotamian underworld) Iran and into Central Asia.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> North Africa, Asia Minor, and the Arabian Peninsula are CLAIMED by Stygia, but that claim came to widespread rebellion by the time of the First Crusades in the Skinlands. With the Second Great Maelstrom they lost control completely. While Maps in Stygia still mark the territory as their own, it is fully under the control of &quot;Heretics&quot; of Muslim, Coptic, and Jewish persuasions; and some call it (unofficially) &quot;the Dark Kingdom of Faith&quot;.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-14T18:19:09-08:00","id":340307,"title":"","content":"Interesting idea and yet another reason why I would <i>love</i> to see a Dark Ages: Wraith book.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://youtu.be/_yBS2EZFduI?t=2m13s\" target=\"_blank\">Homo sapiens. What an inventive, invincible species. It's only a few million years since they crawled up out of the mud and learned to walk. Puny, defenceless bipeds. They've survived flood, famine and plague. They've survived cosmic wars and holocausts. And now, here they are, out among the stars, waiting to begin a new life. Ready to outsit eternity. They're indomitable. Indomitable.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/420-akatsukileader13","name":"AkatsukiLeader13","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar420_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1637"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-14T18:32:23-08:00","id":340316,"title":"","content":"I actually have a whole pitch I need to write up for a Dark Ages setting DURING the Second Great Maelstrom (so the 14th rather than 12/13th Century of most other Dark Ages material - but it'd have the historic info to run games in those eras too).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-14T18:54:21-08:00","id":340349,"title":"","content":"I disagree. I have no problem with the Middle East being part of the Dark Kingdom of Iron. The Middle East or Dar-al-Islam is just as much part of Stygia as Western civilization. BOTH are descended from Classical Civilization and have a Greek cultural inheritance. Even though Persian history is separate from Rome, it is intrinsically tied to the Greek world. The Achaemenid Empire was very involved with Greek civilization and lots of Greeks lived there. It became part of the Hellenestic world with the conquests of Alexander. I have no problem with it being part of Stygia.<br /> <br /> If there is one thing I really hate about late Second Edition/Revised WoD is the introduction of Post-Modern multiculturalism into concepts were they were not appropriate at all. It turned the Camarilla from a worldwide organization of vampires with founders being from Europe, Africa, and India into a European organization. It segregated anything from Asia into some bizarre ghetto where every single supernatural creature there was different from ones found elsewhere. It took twentieth century cultural conceptions and said &quot;This is how it is for all time; actually ignore real history.&quot; It made things that were grand and awesome into lame.<br /> <br /> I like some of the ideas behind the various Dark Kingdoms, but there is not really all that much difference between Middle East and Europe in terms of their conception of the afterlife. Both were originally pagan societies (with lots of cultural transference between the two) supplanted by monotheism with common roots. Any distinction you would want can be obtained through the Far Shores. Instead of Paradise for the Fishers, you have the Garden for Muslims. Maybe different Heretic groups exist in different portions of the Skinlands, but there is no reason for a new Dark Kingdom. It also ignores the fact that the Middle East and North Africa retained a lot of Christian population for a very long time after the Muslim conquests. If you want a specific Dark Kingdom to exist based on Persian influence (presumably Zoroastrian?), then you are looking at Iran, Central Asia, and part of India - NOT North Africa.<br /> <br /> I think the setting's grandeur is diminished once you start injecting post-moden ideas that turns everything into a pale shadow of modern cultural conceptions.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-14T19:21:01-08:00","id":340359,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/340150-missing-dark-kingdom?p=340349#post340349\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> If there is one thing I really hate about late Second Edition/Revised WoD is the introduction of Post-Modern multiculturalism into concepts were they were not appropriate at all. It turned the Camarilla from a worldwide organization of vampires with founders being from Europe, Africa, and India into a European organization. It segregated anything from Asia into some bizarre ghetto where every single supernatural creature there was different from ones found elsewhere. It took twentieth century cultural conceptions and said &quot;This is how it is for all time; actually ignore real history.&quot; It made things that were grand and awesome into lame..</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> By Late Second Edition you mean &quot;Vampire the Masquerade:First Ediition&quot; right? Because The Camarilla and sabbat being pure european things predates... Werewolf as a line. Asia having its own type of stuff.. predates Werewolf as a line.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2715-lian","name":"Lian","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-14T19:38:05-08:00","id":340369,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/340150-missing-dark-kingdom?p=340349#post340349\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Achaemenid Empire was very involved with Greek civilization and lots of Greeks lived there. It became part of the Hellenestic world with the conquests of Alexander.</div> </div> </div> </div>Stygia's rule dates from Rome, and previous editions explicitly tied it's Middle Eastern presence to Roman Conquest - which wouldn't move it further east than Anatolia and the Levant. I agree there are Greek roots in the Muslim world mixed with the Persian - the scholarship of the Abbasid Caliphate makes that clear enough. But Stygia's history has always been distinctly that of the Romanized West (which applies beyond the borders of the original empire thanks to the influence of the Medieval Church[es], but not Arabia and Central Asia.)<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If there is one thing I really hate about late Second Edition/Revised WoD is the introduction of Post-Modern multiculturalism into concepts were they were not appropriate at all. It turned the Camarilla from a worldwide organization of vampires with founders being from Europe, Africa, and India into a European organization. It segregated anything from Asia into some bizarre ghetto where every single supernatural creature there was different from ones found elsewhere. It took twentieth century cultural conceptions and said &quot;This is how it is for all time; actually ignore real history.&quot; It made things that were grand and awesome into lame. </div> </div> </div>The was the CWoD treated the &quot;Mysterious East&quot; (tm) wasn't post-modern multiculturalism. It was old school Orientalism.<br /> <br /> Global (as distinct from purely Imperialist) institutions are a 20th/21st Century phenomenon, so I really have no idea what you're talking about here in terms of &quot;real history&quot;.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If you want a specific Dark Kingdom to exist based on Persian influence (presumably Zoroastrian?), then you are looking at Iran, Central Asia, and part of India - NOT North Africa.<br /> <br /> I think the setting's grandeur is diminished once you start injecting post-moden ideas that turns everything into a pale shadow of modern cultural conceptions. </div> </div> </div>I explicitly said the same thing about the lack of Persian roots in North Africa and Asia Minor, more than once.<br /> <br /> I don't really see how &quot;Muslim Wraiths aren't going to go along with the rule of culturally Western Legions&quot; is a modern cultural conception. One could make that claim easily enough studying only the early Middle Ages!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":1098,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","user":"glamourweaver","date":". 12-14-2014, 10:40 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-14T20:11:09-08:00","id":340412,"title":"","content":"I'm also not sure if I feel it's okay to keep defining the Middle East by it's religious zealotry; it's starting to feel a bit like a tired stereotype.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1136-wolfgar","name":"Wolfgar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1136_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"646"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-14T20:25:51-08:00","id":340443,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wolfgar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/340150-missing-dark-kingdom?p=340412#post340412\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm also not sure if I feel it's okay to keep defining the Middle East by it's religious zealotry; it's starting to feel a bit like a tired stereotype.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's not about zealotry, it's just that it's the region that birthed 3 major Western religions, and the distinction of religious identity has underlay it's historic conflicts with the region Stygia's powerbase is (hence my suggestion it would be born out of the Crusades).<br /> <br /> I am NOT suggesting a culture of Fire-Touched (Werewolf the Forsaken reference) religious-crazies. If anything the region would be a safe haven for spiritual and political dissidents from Stygia's enforced secular imperialism.<br /> <br /> Also that points to why I'm suggesting two different Dark Kingdoms. As the Persian/Mesopotamian one would NOT have the &quot;Heretic rule&quot; element that the &quot;region that Stygia can't control on its border&quot; (North Africa &amp; the Levant) would have.<br /> <br /> Also we're talking about the CWoD, where the &quot;Web of Faith&quot; is already the defining metaphysical trait of the region.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":1098,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","user":"glamourweaver","date":". 12-14-2014, 11:30 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-15T10:21:21-08:00","id":340921,"title":"","content":"I'm personally still going to go with &quot;no blank space&quot;. The area of modern Iraq and Iran (and Pakistan and Afghanistan) is considered to be Stygia and probably is as much as anywhere else that is part of the Empire, but not part of the &quot;core territory&quot; in Europe. The deeds of Alexander and the rule of the Selucids seems as much of a decisive event as to have brought it under the influence of the future Dark Kingdom of Iron. There might be a high incident of Renegades and Heretics there and some of them might be claiming authority based on past and subsequent non-Roman/Stygian influences, but there seems to be enough influence to put it under the heavy hand of Iron. <br /> <br /> However, I don't see any reason NOT to have a Dark Kingdom of &lt;insert something relevant&gt; for the areas of under long-term Persian sway that don't seem to have been gobbled up by adjacent Swar and Stygia. It would certainly make the Near East and adjacent Middle East a more &quot;interesting&quot; place in a way very similar to how it was during Roman and Byzantine times. With a heavy influence of Heretic from the more recent dead to include but not give dominance to the current religions of the region. However, it's going to be a tough row to hoe to figure out what whatever is there is like. Is it more pre-Zoorastrian? (More like a classic Mesopotamian) Or very Zoorastian? Or the later overlaying the former? In any of those cases the sources are going to be hard to get to. Is it going to be more like the Persian Empire? (Which would be my bet, since Stygia is a lot more like the Roman and British Empires than it is like Dis, Hades or Hell) If so, which one? That of Darayava and Xsayarsha? The Parthians? The Sasanian? <br /> <br /> I've always thought that the ancient Dark Kingdoms were sort of &quot;lost&quot;. As has been said, the Dark Kingdom of Sand isn't poaching modern Egyptian wraiths. The don't have the right kind of souls, all two-party (or three, maybe, w/Eidolon) instead of the proper nine. Plus the Dark Kingdom of Sand has a sweet set up for Memoriam that is still generating a steady Pathos flow. But I never really saw it as a place you could just catch the Midnight Express and go to. Or even get to the closest station and have a bit of a hike. Though more the latter possibly than the former. I always got the impression that the old, non-harvesting older Dark Kingdoms were dislocated in a typically wraithly 4-D strange way from the current Shadowlands. Maybe you could catch the Midnight Express to Cairo, but crossing the Nile westward might be problematic as all the necropoli of Kemet of old might be present.... (Or maybe they all &quot;detach&quot; and float off and become Far Shores as they become less relevant to the Skinlands?)<br /> <br /> If that's the case, then the Dark Kingdoms of Obsidian, Clay, Gold and Flint might be pale remnants of their former glory. (Though those are pretty oversimplified visions of the New World and Australia in any case.)There isn't a lot of Pathos flowing to them. They aren't harvesting new wraiths for the most part. And the underworlds/Dark Kingdoms of other cultures would be in similar straits. There probably isn't much of the Mesopotamian Dark Kingdom (of Dust?, considering their view of the afterlife) to get to anymore. Likewise the Dark Kingdom of the Levant (of Mud/Ooze/Phlegm? That's how it's pictured in Canaainte texts, though you get something pretty much like the Mesopotamian one as well when you look at ideas like Sheol). Then you have the Shadowlands of the Hittites, etc. etc. The Dark Kingdom of Sand might be doing as well as it is because of the proclivities and power of the Ancient Egyptians. Those of other cultures might not be doing so hot...<br /> <br /> And, as for Central and Northern Asia... wow... Probably under Stygia? Maybe? I don't think so... But what to do?<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-22T11:59:35-08:00","id":347569,"title":"","content":"My take is that there ought to have BEEN a Dark Kingdom (or Kingdoms) in lands that we now consider the Middle East, but the coming of the Prophet and the advent of Islam changed all of that. The Abrahamic faiths eventually came to define Stygia as it had defined the late Roman Empire and various successor states. So all People of the Book became subject of the Dark Kingdom of Iron, whether they really wanted to do so or not. Such a situation raises some odd questions of just HOW that works around the world, especially when applied in regards to places like Indonesia (where more Muslims live than anywhere else on the globe) or how the 'map of the Underworld' has changed in the past few millennia.<br /> <br /> ...Yeah, the above is, ultimately, MY take on it, and no one else need agree. However, I think that it fits with the setting, even as I'd be chuffed for any alternate explanations!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>B.I.G. Bird spreads the word: Anybody with a heart votes love<br /> <a href=\"https://absintheandthorns.obsidianportal.com/\" target=\"_blank\">https://absintheandthorns.obsidianportal.com/</a></i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/409-phaolan","name":"Phaolan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar409_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"478"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-04T01:16:26-08:00","id":358911,"title":"","content":"Picking up on the ideas in one of the early editions of <b>Mummy</b>, I think it has to do with the Quick (i.e. the living) and &quot;memorium&quot; (the power of memory to feed the dead) writ large. Stygia, the Dark Kingdom of Iron, was founded by Greco-Roman wraiths . . . societies that still fascinate and influence Western civilization. Their empires may have fallen, but their beliefs, culture, and society have endured and inspired. The Romans aspired to be Greeks, the Byzantine and Holy Roman Empire (Germany) tried to be the Romans, then came the Renaissance, when <i>everyone</i> was trying to be the Greeks <i>and</i> the Romans . . .on until today. We live in societies governed under (modified) Greek democractic or Roman republican style governments, debate classic Greco-Roman thinkers, perform their plays . .. And just how many buildings have Greek columns in front?<br /> <br /> Heck, I even had one history professor who argued that the sole purpose of Western civilization was to hold onto Greek culture and Roman law. That may be hyperbole, but it still reflects a <i>massive</i> amount of memorium flowing to the Underworld, and its never truly ended. If anything, its probably increased, as Western societies spread to the Americas . . . So it makes sense that Stygia would also grow and prosper. Why else would North American wraiths end up there? <br /> <br /> I'm not as familiar with Chinese history, but I believe similar circumstances prevail there. So it makes sense the Dark Kingdom of Jade would prosper, too. <br /> <br /> But consider Amenti, Dark Kingdom of Sand. According to <i>Mummy</i>, it was once a major player . . . but has fallen into ruin and been eclipsed by Stygia over the years. Few go there anymore, except the Shemsu-heru and Ishmaelites (the Egyptian mummies). And that makes sense. Who wants to be a part of the ancient Egyptian afterlife? Spending all day, every day of your life toiling for your god-king, just so you can die . . . and do the exact same thing again in the afterlife, for all eternity? Amenti is sustained by a trickle of memorium from tourists who want to see the Pyramids, and the odd individual who decides to have his body mummified. <br /> <br /> By that logic, there's probably a Dark Kingdom for every culture that ever existed . . . assuming they haven't dissolved back into the Tempest. But as the Quick abandon the beliefs and practices that once fueled it, and wraiths stop going there, the Dark Kingdom slowly disappears from the scene. There's probably a fallen Dark Kingdom for the Norse, the Celts, and so on. Meet a Ferryman's price, he can probably still take you there. But they're not going to be competing for Necropoli in the Shadowlands, nor marching to war anytime soon (or ever)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4624-one-vorlon","name":"One Vorlon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4624_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"537"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-04T13:52:41-08:00","id":359506,"title":"","content":"Using your logic, it is hard to argue that the Dark Kingdom of Sand wouldn't be a pretty significant presence. People may implicitly be respecting some institutions and art styles centuries removed from their originals and granting some Memoriam, but... people are explicitly fascinated with the artifacts of the Egyptian afterlife. No intermediary meanings or attributions. THe Great Pyramid is a TOMB and everyone knows it. Mummies, mummies everywhere. Lots of odd looking art that shows up, gets transported around. Etc. Etc. The direct amount of Memoriam has got to be HUGE (and has been for quite some time).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-05T05:20:40-08:00","id":360131,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>glamourweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/340150-missing-dark-kingdom?p=340301#post340301\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">North Africa, Asia Minor, and the Arabian Peninsula are CLAIMED by Stygia, but that claim came to widespread rebellion by the time of the First Crusades in the Skinlands. With the Second Great Maelstrom they lost control completely. While Maps in Stygia still mark the territory as their own, it is fully under the control of &quot;Heretics&quot; of Muslim, Coptic, and Jewish persuasions; and some call it (unofficially) &quot;the Dark Kingdom of Faith&quot;.</div> </div> </div> </div>while being an interesting idea, I think it is unlikely<br /> while a religious man isn't stricly a fanatic and therefore can be a reasonable interlocutor, an organization (like an &quot;heretic&quot; government) based around a religion would become necessarily fanatic in nature. This and the claustrophobic nature of the Underworld, with the constant threat of Oblivion and the Shadow-eaten can easily transform all the little &quot;heretic&quot; states in beliggerent city-states, interested in taking down their neighbours because they don't know if they are Shadow-Eaten or not, or simply don't agree with their particular vision of afterlife. And in this marasmic situation, the monolithic Dark Kingdom of Iron can probably profit of the situation, taking the city-states back, one by one (we can add that only one Dark Kingdom is religion-based, in the Wraith world, and that is Swar, so we can see where the authors are pointing).<br /> So, while is an interesting idea for a scenario, I don't think it could be a durable situation (maybe more a transitory situation, located between the XII and the XIV century).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>====================================<br /> &quot;Worst English-language User&quot; 2011-2015, 2017 running for the 2018<br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><br /> <br /> <img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://i.imgur.com/Ed6RV6Z.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></div></div> </div> </div>","edited":{"userid":515,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/515-ryoga84","user":"Ryoga84","date":". 01-05-2015, 08:48 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/515-ryoga84","name":"Ryoga84","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar515_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"552"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-17T04:18:08-07:00","id":445568,"title":"","content":"In my opinion, it makes much more internal sense to view the DK as units of culture first, and land second. The DKoI thus encompasses all regions with a strong monotheistic tint and a worldview clumsily derived from greco-roman and germanic principles.<br /> The DKoI thus controls the ME _now_ not because the romans held the area for less then a century, but because the monotheism/mystery cult mix that dominates or dominated the local spiritual outlook. There were other DK over there before the monotheistic takeover, but those are underground cults, with little power to reap new souls.<br /> <br /> Even in canon, the DKoI is less a centralized Kingdom, and more a feudal state with the authority of the Deathlords getting weaker and weaker as you go &quot;further&quot; from Stygia and its core Necropoli. It is entirely possible that the Middle East Necropoli have slightly different rules, that their local narratives are more overtly religious (Islamic, probably. Perhaps there are wars between Sunni and Shia heretics), and have some influence from the Indian Shadowlands.<br /> <br /> Alternatively, just come up with a localized DK that is a vassal to the DKoI, and is tolerated to do as it pleases as long as x% of souls end up in the DKoI, as manpower or soulsteel.<br /> <br /> <br /> (Sidenote/rant: it is only due to the grecofetishism of the Renaissance that we consider our current governmental structure to be derived from the greeks and our culture from the romans. In reality, the roots of current democracy are not in ancient Greece (seeing as how they decried democracy as mob rule), but in the freeman assemblies of germanic societies, and the free burgher self-governance derived from there. It bugs me sometimes how much this uncritical fetishism for Athens and Rome still permeates our culture. Rant over <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":5238,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5238-herbert_west","user":"Herbert_West","date":". 03-17-2015, 07:25 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5238-herbert_west","name":"Herbert_West","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5238_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"473"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-17T06:00:57-07:00","id":445686,"title":"","content":"Frankly, it seems daft to assume that Greek culture and Roman democracy, in the form of Stygia, would come to the Underworld and usurp native cultures that had existing for millennia, despite the fact that even now Greek culture and Roman democracy aren't particularly popular concepts in the region, just because. The Middle East had culture while we were still banging the rocks together (I exaggerate, but you get the point). I find it very hard to believe that early Mesopotamian or even later Persian rulers wouldn't have built their own empires and cemented their rule, as did Charon in the West and Yu Huang in the East *millennia later*. The mythical figure of Gilgamesh, for instance, would be a prime candidate. He was king of Uruk and is remembered today, so would receive at least a trickle of memoriam from scholars and poetry readers.<br /> <br /> Let's not forget the pride Middle Easterners have for their own cultures. Iranians have huge pride in their Persian heritage. People still read Rumi and the Sufi poets. Islam is a *huge* cultural force. Therefore the wraiths of that region will still be reaping pathos and memoriam from sources that are specifically tied to Middle Eastern culture--not Western, long-dead cultures that existed in the Middle East for what is essentially a drop in the ocean. Add to this the fact that a predominantly non-religious culture (Stygia) is to be assumed to rule a population for whom religion is often intrinsic to their own culture, and it simply doesn't make sense.<br /> <br /> I like the idea of a Dark Kingdom of Dust. YMMV, of course, but that's the wonder of the internet :P","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer, publisher, performer<br /> Mostly he/his, sometimes she/her IRL <a href=\"https://adam-lowe.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://adam-lowe.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","name":"adambeyoncelowe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-17T06:15:44-07:00","id":445701,"title":"","content":"Also, the first written language was in Sumer, so that would be a key turning point for wraiths who rely on memoriam. And, unlike, the Americas and Australia, the indigenous populations weren't decimated or reduced to a minority. While the DK of Obsidian wouldn't have many pureblood Amerindians to feed them pathos and memoriam, the indigenous peoples of the Middle East are still very much there and in large numbers.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer, publisher, performer<br /> Mostly he/his, sometimes she/her IRL <a href=\"https://adam-lowe.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://adam-lowe.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","name":"adambeyoncelowe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-17T16:35:14-07:00","id":446688,"title":"","content":"I like Dark Kingdom of Dust. A lot. Are you thinking for Persia? Or for Mesopotamia + Persia from Sumer on? (Does that mean that the Canaanite underworld should be the Dark Kindgom of Mud? Phlegm? Can't we have a Dark Kingdom of Snot? Pleeeeeeeease?)<br /> <br /> But I fear it is going to be a tethered quasi-Realm, like Amenti. No matter how cool it would be for Gilgamesh to be running it (exactly like he's supposed to... stuck in the Underworld since he had to die like a man, but feasts with the gods as a god awhen they come to the Underworld). For some reason, Alexander the Great + the Selucids + the Romans gives the area over to Stygia. Stygia seems to just STICK wherever it touches (and beyond, since Persia was NEVER a part of the Roman Empire.... it's got to be Alexander and his successors. <br /> <br /> I fear having Gilgamesh running a part of the underworld might actually be a bit cooler than having Odysseus (or some other Mycenaen hero...) Stygia. Though he would probably be a crap ruler. Running off to have adventures, coming home and demanding massive building projects and teaching hygiene.... He seems more like a movie star.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}